---
// Mission, variant A: "Thesis".
// Three short paragraphs of prose with inline reference markers, and a
// numbered reference list at the foot of the section. Replaces the old
// one-sentence wedge.
import "../../styles/variants/mission.css";
---

<section id="mission" class="py-20 sm:py-28 border-t border-rule">
  <div class="reveal max-w-[840px] mx-auto px-6 sm:px-10">
    <div class="eyebrow mb-6">Mission</div>

    <div class="mission-prose">
      <p>
        <span class="mission-lede"
          >Every agent client re-implements every integration.</span
        > The same API gets wired up again for each new client, with its own auth,
        its own schema handling, and its own failure modes. Credentials end up pasted
        in five places. Nothing shares an idea of what a tool is allowed to do, so
        each client guesses.<a
          class="mission-ref"
          href="#mission-ref-1"
          aria-label="Reference 1">1</a
        >
      </p>

      <p>
        Executor gives every tool one shape. A name, an input schema, an output
        schema. It does not matter whether the tool came from an MCP server, an
        OpenAPI spec, or a GraphQL API. Any agent calls any of them the same
        way. Tools should belong to you, not to one client or one model
        provider.<a
          class="mission-ref"
          href="#mission-ref-2"
          aria-label="Reference 2">2</a
        >
      </p>

      <p>
        The safe path has to be the easy path. Executor keeps the semantics it
        imported: GET against DELETE for OpenAPI, destructiveHint for MCP,
        mutations for GraphQL.<a
          class="mission-ref"
          href="#mission-ref-3"
          aria-label="Reference 3">3</a
        > Calls run in a sandbox. Credentials are attached host-side at call time,
        so a raw token never reaches the sandbox, the agent, or the model.<a
          class="mission-ref"
          href="#mission-ref-4"
          aria-label="Reference 4">4</a
        >
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mission-refs">
      <div class="mission-refs__label">References</div>
      <ol class="mission-refs__list">
        <li id="mission-ref-1">
          <span class="mission-refs__n">[1]</span>
          <span
            ><a
              href="https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/code-execution-with-mcp"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">Code execution with MCP</a
            > <span class="mission-refs__src">Anthropic, 2025</span></span
          >
        </li>
        <li id="mission-ref-2">
          <span class="mission-refs__n">[2]</span>
          <span
            ><a
              href="https://blog.cloudflare.com/code-mode/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">Code Mode</a
            > <span class="mission-refs__src">Cloudflare</span></span
          >
        </li>
        <li id="mission-ref-3">
          <span class="mission-refs__n">[3]</span>
          <span
            ><a
              href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/tools"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener">Tools, tool annotations</a
            > <span class="mission-refs__src"
              >Model Context Protocol, 2025-06-18</span
            ></span
          >
        </li>
        <li id="mission-ref-4">
          <span class="mission-refs__n">[4]</span>
          <span
            ><a href="https://executor.sh/docs">Sandboxing and credentials</a>
            <span class="mission-refs__src">Executor docs</span></span
          >
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
